      "title": "Administrative Assistant-Occupational Therapy (.5 FTE)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nJob Description To provide comprehensive administrative and operational support to the School of Health Professions (SHP), with primary responsibility for the Department of Medical Science, including the Master of Science in Physician Assistant Medicine program, and the Department of Occupational Therapy, including the Doctor of Occupational Therapy program. This is a cross-departmental position designed to enhance administrative capacity, improve coordination and communication, and support the effective operation and continued development of both programs. The position coordinates information and activities among students, faculty, department leadership, administrators, internal College departments, clinical partners, accrediting bodies, and other external agencies. The position is responsible for developing, managing, maintaining, and supporting administrative, operational, regulatory, assessment, and programmatic processes within the School of Health Professions. Required Qualifications - Three years of job-related experience. - Demonstrated ability to work within a team. - Strong written and oral communication skills. - Cultural competence in effectively working with a diverse population. - Understanding of and willingness to embrace the Benedictine values. - Ability to simultaneously manage and coordinate a large number of varying tasks. - Proficient in data management and computer skills - Basic office skills required: keyboarding, filing, and answering telephones. Preferred Qualifications - Bachelor’s Degree - Experience in project support - Experience in grant support - At least five years of successful office or administrative support experience -Proficiency with electronic record keeping and office organizational tools (software)",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=f49f191a-7524-465b-8442-e0768f85867a&ccId=31713375_2&jobId=57822709_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Student Accounts Counselor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nThe approximate base compensation range is posted, but the actual offer will reflect our total compensation and benefits package. The offer will be determined by a number of factors including the selected candidate’s experience, knowledge, skills, abilities, FTE, number of months worked, as well as internal equity among our team. Job Summary: Process timely and accurate student account billing functions, including third-party billing and processing student refunds. Manage and support student payment plans.  Contribute to a positive student experience by providing prompt and professional customer service.  Complete regular general ledger account reconciliations.  Review and analyze account transactions for discrepancies and errors.  Perform necessary research to resolve outstanding and unreconciled transactions.  Provide, maintain, and review required documentation for audit compliance.  Monitor electronic banking activity for returned student payments and refunds to ensure timely posting of transactions into the general ledger.  Prepare and post journal vouchers.  Work with colleagues across departments to resolve student account billing questions and issues.  Provide timely and accurate information to support reporting and information requests.  Ensure compliance with institutional procedures and policies and all regulations.  Provide functional support for accounts receivable activities as needed.  Perform financial research and analysis projects as directed and compose reports and recommendations as requested.  Assist the Finance department with processing activities as needed and provide administrative support as required.  Assist with financial audits to ensure accurate financial statements and adherence to generally accepted accounting principles; organize and analyze audit schedules and records for accuracy and adherence to accounting standards.  Contribute to the efficiency and effectiveness of the department's service to its customers by offering suggestions and actively participating as a member of work teams.  Key Result & Responsibility: Required Qualifications: Two (2) years of college-level coursework in accounting or a related field AND three (3) years of accounting-related experience; OR an equivalent combination of applicable work experience.  Knowledge of principles and practices of accounting and auditing financial documentation and transactions.  Ability to perform technical, specialized, and complex financial support work.  Ability to read, comprehend, and explain policies and procedures.  Capability to perform independent research and analysis to resolve issues.  Maintain accurate financial records and prepare accurate and timely reports.  Ability to organize work, multitask, identify and set priorities, and meet deadlines.  Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree in Accounting.  Experience in higher education working in student accounts/Bursar’s Office.  Experience with Ellucian BANNER or a similar student information system.  Experience working in a customer service environment.  Ability to utilize tact, discretion, and independent judgment within established guidelines.  Ability to take a leadership role, demonstrate initiative, and solve problems effectively.  Proficient in solving problems and processing anomaly transactions.  Experience with Microsoft Office and Google Suite applications.  Effective communication skills in both oral and written forms.  Ability to contribute effectively to the accomplishment of team goals, objectives, and activities.  Experience interacting successfully with diverse individuals from various backgrounds. – Required Certificates, Licenses, and Registrations: Continued employment is contingent upon all required licenses and certificates being maintained in active status without suspension or revocation",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=f49f191a-7524-465b-8442-e0768f85867a&ccId=31713375_2&jobId=57783370_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director of Student Financial Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nThe approximate base compensation range is posted, but the actual offer will reflect our total compensation and benefits package. The offer will be determined by a number of factors including the selected candidate’s experience, knowledge, skills, abilities, FTE, number of months worked, as well as internal equity among our team. The Director of Student Financial Services provides strategic vision, regulatory compliance oversight, and operational leadership for both the Financial Aid and Student Accounts Offices at The College of St. Scholastica. This critical leadership role integrates financial aid delivery and student account billing/collections into a seamless, student-centered experience. The Director ensures the equitable, accurate, and timely distribution of federal, state, and institutional funds while managing student billing, revenue collections, and account reconciliations. As a key member of the Enrollment Management leadership team, this position oversees the full lifecycle of student financial services for both undergraduate and graduate populations, stewarding student aid while optimizing operational effectiveness across all financial touchpoints. Essential Duties & Responsibilities Strategic Leadership & Enrollment Integration Strategic Oversight: Provide overarching strategic direction for both Financial Aid and Student Accounts, ensuring alignment with institutional enrollment, retention, and affordability goals and leading the Student Accounts Office through student billing, payment, refunds and accounts. Aid Leveraging & Revenue Strategy: Partner with the Associate VP for Enrollment Management and external partners to analyze discount rates, net tuition revenue, and packaging models to support recruitment and retention targets. Budgets & Packaging: Collaborate with institutional leadership to establish annual aid budgets, merit scholarship parameters, need-based aid philosophies, and revenue collection targets. Funnel & Yield Support: Partner with Admissions to shape clear affordability messaging, deliver timely financial aid awards and billing projections, and participate in yield initiatives. Cross-Departmental Collaboration: Work closely with Admissions, Registrar, Finance, IT, Student Affairs and Academic Affairs to ensure data integrity, streamline cross-functional workflows, and eliminate financial barriers to persistence. Regulatory Compliance, Audits & Reporting Primary Authority: Serve as the institutional expert on Title IV regulations, Minnesota Office of Higher Education (MOHE) programs, FERPA, and higher education billing and collection mandates. Audit & Reconciliation: Direct all internal and external compliance reviews, program reviews, and annual financial aid/fiscal audits. Ensure complete reconciliation between financial aid disbursements, student account ledger postings, and finance office records. Mandatory Reporting: Oversee the accurate and timely submission of state and federal reports Policy Administration: Maintain, update, and uniformly enforce institutional policies regarding Satisfactory Academic Progress (SAP), Return of Title IV Funds (R2T4), student billing, refunding, and credit/adjustment allowances. Student Accounts & Business Operations Billing & Collections Management: Direct operations related to student account generation, fee assessment, third-party billing, payment plans, refunds, and collections strategies. Receivables & Aging Analysis: Monitor account aging, evaluate trend data, oversee collection agency workflows, and provide regular reports to senior leadership regarding account performance and outstanding balances. Appeals & Escalations: Act as the final point of escalation for complex financial aid cases, professional judgment determinations, dependency overrides, and high-level student account or fee disputes. Systems Integration & Process Improvement Student Information Systems Transition: Lead the strategic management and operational transition across both Financial Aid and Student Accounts, including system configuration, testing, and workflow optimization. Data Integrity: Ensure seamless integration and data synchronization across systems. Continuous Improvement: Regularly evaluate operational procedures, identify efficiencies, and leverage technology solutions to automate processes, enhance accuracy, and improve student-facing services. Qualifications Minimum Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Accounting, Finance, Higher Education Administration, or a closely related field. Leadership: Minimum of three (3) years of direct supervisory experience leading professional teams and driving process improvement. Technical Proficiency: Demonstrated experience managing student information systems (SIS), database packaging, financial reporting, and/or data analysis (including MS Excel). Preferred Qualifications Education: Master’s degree from an accredited institution. Institution Type: Experience in a private, liberal arts college environment supporting both undergraduate and graduate student populations. Strategic Enrollment: Background in financial aid leveraging, net tuition revenue modeling, and data-driven enrollment planning. Professional Engagement: Active involvement or leadership roles in professional organizations  Working Conditions & Physical Requirements Primary Setting: Professional office environment operating primarily during standard business hours, with extended hours required during peak periods (e.g., registration, term roll, census, and audit windows). Interpersonal Contact: Frequent collaboration with internal departments, executive leadership, external regulatory agencies, and auditors. Travel: Occasional travel required for regional or national conferences, regulatory training sessions, and professional development.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=f49f191a-7524-465b-8442-e0768f85867a&ccId=31713375_2&jobId=57758633_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Advancement Officer for Athletics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-02\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nAdvancement Officer for Athletics  The Advancement Officer for Athletics (AOA) supports the College's efforts to grow Athletic gift and sponsorship revenue by working with alumni, parents, friends, corporations, and prospective donors who have the potential to support strategic objectives of the College pertaining to athletics.  The AOA reports to the Executive Director of Development with a dual reporting line to the Director of Athletics and Recreation. The position works collaboratively within the Office of College Advancement, which includes Development, Alumni Engagement, Advancement Services, and Foundation and Government Resources .  Responsibilities:  Serve as Advancement staff lead with the Athletics Department Identify, cultivate, and solicit sponsorships and gift support for Athletics by developing and maintaining relationships with corporations, foundations, and individuals, including alumni, faculty, staff, local and regional businesses;  Build collaborative working relationships and serve as development liaison to coaches, College departments, and other key personnel to achieve successful fundraising; Responsible for a portfolio of major sponsorship and gift donors as assigned by the Executive Director of Development and the Director of Athletics and Recreation; Collaborate with the Annual Giving team and departmental colleagues in successful planning and execution of Athletics Week of Giving and other giving days;  Attend Advancement staff and prospect management team meetings, as well as key Athletic Department meetings;  Develop competency in fundraising and alumni engagement best practices through professional development;  Up to 20% of time, the AOA will support operations of the Athletic Department in areas including, but not limited to, strategic planning, NCAA compliance, sport oversight, budget development and management, and student-athlete welfare.  Demonstrate commitment to Inclusive Excellence as an expression of the Catholic Benedictine Values and identity of the College;  Travel and weekend/evening work as required to fulfill job responsibilities. Required Qualifications:  ●  Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience  ●  Excellent written and interpersonal communication skills  ●  Ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines simultaneously  ●  Experience with office software  ●  Ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse communities and cultures  ●  Ability to travel throughout the United States Preferred Qualifications:  ●  Previous fundraising experience working directly with donors  ●  Knowledge of Division III athletics and particularly the MIAC conference  ●  Understanding of fundraising techniques  ●  Knowledge of tax guidelines regarding charitable giving  The College of St. Scholastica  Advancement Officer for Athletics  The Advancement Officer for Athletics (AOA) supports the College's efforts to grow Athletic gift and sponsorship revenue by working with alumni, parents, friends, corporations, and prospective donors who have the potential to support strategic objectives of the College pertaining to athletics.  The AOA reports to the Executive Director of Development with a dual reporting line to the Director of Athletics and Recreation. The position works collaboratively within the Office of College Advancement, which includes Development, Alumni Engagement, Advancement Services, and Foundation and Government Resources .  Responsibilities:  Serve as Advancement staff lead with the Athletics Department Identify, cultivate, and solicit sponsorships and gift support for Athletics by developing and maintaining relationships with corporations, foundations, and individuals, including alumni, faculty, staff, local and regional businesses;  Build collaborative working relationships and serve as development liaison to coaches, College departments, and other key personnel to achieve successful fundraising; Responsible for a portfolio of major sponsorship and gift donors as assigned by the Executive Director of Development and the Director of Athletics and Recreation; Collaborate with the Annual Giving team and departmental colleagues in successful planning and execution of Athletics Week of Giving and other giving days;  Attend Advancement staff and prospect management team meetings, as well as key Athletic Department meetings;  Develop competency in fundraising and alumni engagement best practices through professional development;  Up to 20% of time, the AOA will support operations of the Athletic Department in areas including, but not limited to, strategic planning, NCAA compliance, sport oversight, budget development and management, and student-athlete welfare.  Demonstrate commitment to Inclusive Excellence as an expression of the Catholic Benedictine Values and identity of the College;  Travel and weekend/evening work as required to fulfill job responsibilities. Required Qualifications:  ●  Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience  ●  Excellent written and interpersonal communication skills  ●  Ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines simultaneously  ●  Experience with office software  ●  Ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse communities and cultures  ●  Ability to travel throughout the United States Preferred Qualifications:  ●  Previous fundraising experience working directly with donors  ●  Knowledge of Division III athletics and particularly the MIAC conference  ●  Understanding of fundraising techniques  ●  Knowledge of tax guidelines regarding charitable giving  Physical Demands & Working Environment:  The physical demands described herein are representative of those an employee must meet to successfully perform the essential functions of the job. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions.  Mobility to work in a typical office setting and use standard office equipment; vision to read printed materials and a computer screen; hearing and speech to communicate in person or over the telephone; strength to lift and carry up to 20 pounds.  EEO Statement  The College of St. Scholastica is an equal opportunity employer committed to creating an educational and work environment that is supportive of all students, faculty, and staff. The College is committed to building a robust athletics community that reflects the students and communities we serve. Candidates with experiences and perspectives from all backgrounds that contribute to this goal are encouraged to apply.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=f49f191a-7524-465b-8442-e0768f85867a&ccId=31713375_2&jobId=60693996_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Purchasing Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nThe approximate base compensation range is posted, but the actual offer will reflect our total compensation and benefits package. The offer will be determined by a number of factors including the selected candidate’s experience, knowledge, skills, abilities, FTE, number of months worked, as well as internal equity among our team. Position Summary The Purchasing Manager oversees the College’s purchasing, procurement, insurance coordination, and related business services. This position promotes responsible financial stewardship through effective purchasing practices, supplier and contract management, insurance administration, and continuous process improvement. The Purchasing Manager will play an important role in supporting the implementation, integration, and ongoing use of Workday for procurement and related business processes. Serving as a primary resource for departments across campus, the Manager provides guidance on purchasing policies, workflows, system functionality, vendor relationships, and insurance requirements while identifying opportunities to improve efficiency, accountability, and customer service. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Purchasing and Procurement: Oversee purchasing activities and coordinate the timely, cost-effective acquisition of goods and services. Evaluate suppliers, negotiate pricing and terms, coordinate orders and deliveries, and identify opportunities for cost savings and improved purchasing efficiency. Workday Integration and Systems: Support the implementation, configuration, testing, and ongoing optimization of Workday purchasing and procurement processes. Help establish effective workflows, maintain accurate supplier and purchasing data, support system adoption and user training, and collaborate with Finance, Information Technology, and campus departments to improve system functionality, reporting, and internal controls. Vendor and Contract Management: Establish and maintain supplier relationships, monitor vendor performance, coordinate contract administration and renewals, and collaborate with departments to resolve purchasing issues and assess vendor-related risks. Insurance Coordination: Coordinate insurance documentation, certificates of insurance, renewals, and related requirements with brokers, insurers, vendors, and internal departments. Maintain accurate records, monitor deadlines, and assist with insurance claims and information requests. Business Operations and Process Improvement: Develop and maintain purchasing procedures, strengthen internal controls, analyze purchasing activity, and recommend improvements that support cost containment, compliance, and operational effectiveness. Campus Engagement and Customer Service: Serve as a knowledgeable and responsive resource for faculty, staff, and administrators. Provide guidance on procurement policies, Workday processes, purchasing procedures, and related business services while promoting consistent practices across campus. Reporting and Administration: Maintain accurate procurement, vendor, contract, and insurance records. Use Workday, Excel, and other information systems to analyze purchasing activity, prepare reports, monitor performance, and support informed decision-making. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in Business Management, Business Administration, Procurement, Supply Chain Management, or a related field, and at least three years of professional experience in purchasing, procurement, or business operations, preferably in higher education;  or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Strong analytical, organizational, problem-solving, communication, and negotiation skills. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel, and the ability to work effectively with databases, enterprise systems, and electronic records. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, exercise sound judgment, and perform effectively in a fast-paced environment. Strong interpersonal skills and a commitment to customer service, collaboration, and continuous improvement. Demonstrated cultural competence and the ability to work effectively with individuals from diverse backgrounds and experiences. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, nonprofit organizations, or complex institutional environments. Experience with Workday or another enterprise resource planning (ERP) system, including procurement workflows, system implementation, testing, reporting, or user training. Familiarity with supplier negotiations, contract administration, vendor management, and purchasing policies. Experience coordinating insurance documentation, certificates of insurance, renewals, or general risk management activities. The Purchasing Manager supports the College’s operational effectiveness and financial sustainability through efficient procurement, effective Workday utilization, strong vendor relationships, coordinated insurance activities, and responsible stewardship of institutional resources.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=f49f191a-7524-465b-8442-e0768f85867a&ccId=31713375_2&jobId=60661884_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Alumni Engagement Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nThe approximate base compensation range is posted, but the actual offer will reflect our total compensation and benefits package. The offer will be determined by a number of factors including the selected candidate’s experience, knowledge, skills, abilities, FTE, number of months worked, as well as internal equity among our team. ORGANIZATIONAL OVERVIEW College Advancement supports The College of St. Scholastica's mission and vision by facilitating strategic relationships with alumni, parents, friends, businesses, donors, government agencies, and private foundations. POSITION SUMMARY The Alumni Engagement Officer is pivotal in cultivating a vibrant, informed, and actively involved alumni community. This position serves as the primary storyteller and communications architect for alumni relations, creating compelling multi-channel content to elevate connection and philanthropy. In addition, working in close collaboration with the Director of Alumni Engagement, the Officer leads the coordination and execution of dynamic alumni events, maintains strategic alumni and parent relationships, and fulfills key operational duties supporting the advancement mission of the College. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES 1. Communications & Content Creation (40%) Lead the overall strategic direction, creation, writing, editing, and distribution of alumni communications across print, digital, and social media channels to strengthen alumni connection, highlight philanthropic impact, and drive active engagement. Multi-Channel Content Strategy & Execution: Lead the creation, writing, and editing of high-impact alumni communications across various channels, including digital publications, social media platforms, website news, email campaigns, and print media. CSS Magazine & Class Notes Management: Prepare, solicit, write, and format all submitted class notes, birth, death, and sympathy announcements, as well as high-resolution photo submissions for inclusion in the CSS Magazine. Cross-Departmental Collaboration: Work closely with the central Marketing and Communications team to align messaging, enforce brand standards, and elevate compelling alumni stories that demonstrate institutional ROI and Benedictine values. Digital Media & Campaign Analytics: Manage alumni social media channels and digital communications campaigns, creating dynamic copy and visual content while tracking open rates, click-through rates, and audience engagement metrics to continuously refine content strategy. 2. Event Coordination (30%) Strategic Event Management: Manage key signature events, including major reunion weekends, 'Lighting a Tradition,' regional/geographic-specific alumni gatherings, and networking receptions. Logistics & Facility Coordination: Determine detailed space, technology, equipment, catering, and on-site management requirements based on historical data and strategic goals. Oversee all aspects of venue selection, lighting, sound, room setups, and staffing. Vendor Negotiation & Contract Management: Negotiate contracts with vendors, facilities, caterers, and service providers, ensuring agreements meet high-quality standards and align with event objectives. Budget Optimization & Financial Accountability: Monitor, manage, and optimize event budgets to ensure cost-effectiveness, fiscal responsibility, and optimal resource allocation. Post-Event Evaluation & Quality Improvement: Conduct thorough evaluations of each event by gathering attendee feedback and analyzing key performance metrics to measure effectiveness against annual fundraising and engagement goals. Document lessons learned and implement continuous process improvements. 3. Alumni/Parent Engagement & Relationship Management (20%) Portfolio Management & Outreach: Maintain an active portfolio of alumni and parents, fostering strong, lasting relationships through regular direct personal contact, phone calls, text messages, social media engagement, and strategic email outreach. Student-to-Alumni Lifecycle: Oversee the student-to-alumni transition lifecycle, partnering with key internal stakeholders (Admissions, Student Affairs, and the Center for Experiential Learning) to deliver strategic programming with measurable ROI. Donor & Community Cultivation: Foster and maintain strong relationships with alumni/parents and community partners, utilizing events and communications platforms to cultivate ongoing engagement and philanthropic interest. Event & On-site Representation: Support institutional engagement goals by representing the Advancement Office and attending strategic campus and regional events as needed. Commitment to Inclusive Excellence: Demonstrate a steadfast commitment to inclusive excellence, ensuring that all donor and alumni relations efforts reflect Benedictine values and serve the diverse community of The College of St. Scholastica. 4. Duties as Assigned (10%) Special Projects & Initiatives: Participate in and support institutional, departmental, or cross-functional special projects as assigned by the Director of Alumni Engagement or Advancement Leadership. Operational & Team Support: Provide operational support during peak advancement cycles, major campaign launches, or college-wide celebrations. Professional Development & General Duties: Perform other duties and responsibilities as assigned in alignment with departmental goals and evolving advancement priorities. QUALIFICATIONS Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Communications, Marketing, Business Management, or related field.  Exceptional project management skills with the demonstrated ability to handle multiple complex projects simultaneously. Strong planning and execution skills with excellent attention to detail. Collaborative work style and proven ability to interact effectively with staff, faculty, leadership, and external stakeholders at all levels. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with demonstrated talent in storytelling and content creation across media. Excellent critical thinking, strategic thinking, and execution skills. Experience cultivating and developing positive relationships with individuals, organizations, and businesses. Valid driver's license. Ability to work effectively with diverse communities, cultures, and populations. Flexibility to work a non-traditional schedule, including frequent evenings and weekends as required by event and travel schedules. Willingness and ability to travel domestically and, on occasion, internationally. Preferred Qualifications: Two (2) years of experience in events management, donor stewardship, communications, or alumni relations (OR an equivalent combination of education, training, and experience). Experience with or knowledge of various event models, digital marketing tools, and fundraising strategies. Experience working with advancement and CRM software such as Raiser's Edge, Slate, or similar platforms. Experience working in a higher education or non-profit fundraising environment. Alumna/us of The College of St. Scholastica.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=f49f191a-7524-465b-8442-e0768f85867a&ccId=31713375_2&jobId=46249065_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Library Circulation & Student Employment Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\n\nLocation: Duluth\n\nThe approximate base compensation range is posted, but the actual offer will reflect our total compensation and benefits package. The offer will be determined by a number of factors including the selected candidate’s experience, knowledge, skills, abilities, FTE, number of months worked, as well as internal equity among our team. Shift: Afternoon/Evening Job Description: The Circulation Supervisor works to provide patrons with guidance and research assistance during afternoon and evening hours. This position manages student employees assigned to the desk during scheduled work hours, processes interlibrary lending and borrowing requests, resolves circulation issues, and responds to patron or building safety, maintenance, and security concerns. Essential Job Functions: Circulation Duties:  Oversees circulation activities; provides patrons with instructions or reference assistance in finding and retrieving library materials; lends, renews, and returns materials via Alma, including tracking and issuing fines; assists patrons with questions about resource management, online account management issues, printing complications, and item location. Communicates with patrons to extend, renew, or bill overdue materials, and troubleshoot missing, overdue, expired, unfilled requests. Hire, Train, and Manage the Library’s Work Study Students: In collaboration with others on the library team, hires and provides training, directions, support, and supervision to the student employees.   Interlibrary Loan Fulfillment:  Processes interlibrary loan requests through MINITEX and OCLC; submits borrowing requests, fills lending requests; packs, ships, and unpacks physical items in accordance with delivery systems utilized by the library; performs record-keeping to track transactions; follows up with libraries and patrons to extend, renew, or bill materials, and troubleshoot missing, overdue, expired, unfilled requests. Stacks Maintenance:  Ensures books are shelved and other library materials are returned to their proper shelving locations. Collaborates with the library team to conduct periodic stacks inventory. Building Management:  Opens and/or closes the library based on scheduled work hours (which follow the academic calendar); ensures furniture is orderly and well maintained; addresses and documents any emergency, maintenance, or housekeeping situations, and maintains a direct and effective relationship with campus security. Other Duties:  Participates in staff meetings and training; may receive assignments from other library team members and may be required to perform other duties that support library activities and the mission of the college, such as assisting with purchasing; participates in MnPALS user groups and activities. Because this person supervises student workers, the position will maintain an on-call role on weekends for emergencies and staffing shortages. If hours are accumulated over a weekend, the weekday schedule will be adjusted accordingly. Education: Required: High school diploma Preferred: Associate’s or Bachelor’s degree Professional Experience: Required: Experience working with young adults and/or working in a public-facing service environment. Preferred: Library experience in a Circulation or Access Services area, including experience with Alma or other integrated library systems.",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
